Open a sandbox's windows behind what is shown, and never focus them

niri tells a sandbox's client apart, one connected through a
wp-security-context-v1 socket, only to hide the privileged globals
from it. Its windows open as anyone's do: on the focused workspace,
focused when its rules, an activation token or niri's smart policy
say so. No window rule can single it out either, since rules match
only what the client says of itself. So a fenced agent's window would
take Joel's place and his keys.

A sandbox's new window now opens on the empty workspace at the end of
the focused output, one added after it when Joel is on that one, and
never activates, whatever its rules or tokens say. A workspace that
went or came into view before it maps is chosen again. It never takes
a workspace OpenNextWindowOn asked for an app ID, since it names its
app ID itself; it ignores a parent, so a dialog stays with it; and it
goes fullscreen where it is rather than onto another output.

The workspace is added at the window's first configure, so a window
that never maps still leaves it: while Joel is on the last empty
workspace he briefly has two empty ones after it. That is no leak;
niri removes the spare one as he moves away, as it does any empty
workspace not at the end.

Its xdg-activation marks a window urgent at most: a token a sandbox
makes activates no window, its own or another client's, now or when
that window maps, and a sandbox's own window isn't activated by any
token. It adds no setting, since a sandbox's connection is what
decides it.

